\section{A baseline is a point on a frontier}

An interpreter can start as soon as the input is ready to execute. A baseline compiler delays some work to generate native code but should remove enough dispatch and representation overhead to repay that delay quickly. An optimizing compiler spends more time on analysis, transformation and validation; it is profitable only if enough suitable work remains. ``Baseline'' describes the cost objective, not a requirement that each opcode emit exactly one fixed sequence. In an interactive service, the right comparison is time from arrival to response, including lazy compilation, not native throughput measured after warm-up.

Ben L. Titzer's \emph{Whose baseline compiler is it anyway?} studies single-pass WebAssembly compilers, including Wizard-SPC. A forward walk tracks abstract stack entries as constants, registers or frame slots. Thus a local read can be a change to compiler state rather than a load, and an add with a constant can use an immediate. Wasm's structured control flow lets the compiler see the incoming branches of an ordinary block exit before it emits the exit code. Loop headers are different: unseen backedges require a conservative entry layout. Naively copying an entire locals-and-stack model at each split can make a nominally single-pass compiler expensive. Limit the size of saved states or record changes to them.

\section{A stack machine with a callable baseline}

Suppose a typed bytecode defines addition modulo \(2^{32}\). The prototype below uses a shared frame ABI: the interpreter stores tagged argument slots, and the baseline entry assumes bytecode verification has established that the arguments here are \texttt{i32}. The native register names in the listing are abstract.

\begin{verbatim}
bytecode sum3(x):
  local.get 0      # compiler stack = [slot(0)]
  i32.const 3      # compiler stack = [slot(0), const(3)]
  i32.add          # load payload r0, slot(0); add32 r0, 3
  return           # return i32 r0 according to VM ABI

on_call(f, args):
  v = dispatch[f].load_and_pin()
  if v.tier == INTERP and saturating_increment(calls[f]) >= 64:
    try_enqueue_once(f, v.revision, BASELINE)
  return invoke_and_unpin(v, args)
\end{verbatim}

No interpreter dispatch remains in the native body. For a branch whose target expects a value in a different location, emit a parallel move on the incoming edge, using a scratch location if moves form a cycle. The entry invariant is that at every reachable block the native layout represents exactly the bytecode operand stack and live locals specified for that block. If a helper can allocate, its call site must report live references in the agreed locations; an integer payload is not a root. Test the sample at \(2^{32}-1\), expecting \(2\), and test a diamond with a different location for the same stack slot on each edge. Route both edges through a helper that can collect to catch a misplaced root as well as a misplaced value.

