\subsection{Choose a Fixed Baseline Layout for a Loop Header}

At a structured loop header the baseline compiler has yet to see
the backedge. Instead of keeping a patchable set of register agreements,
choose the entry layout before emitting the body. In this toy VM
each typed loop parameter has a dedicated canonical frame slot;
locals used across edges also have canonical slots. Incoming paths
may temporarily compute parameters in registers or other slots.
Reconcile their layouts by parallel moves, with enough scratch
for cycles. Only the layout contract is fixed at compile time;
the runtime values in those places can differ each iteration.

\begin{sudocode}[Build a header seen before its backedges]
\State allocate typed parameter slots for header $H$
\State record their order as $layout[H]$ \Comment{Independent of path}
\State emit incoming-edge moves into $layout[H]$
\State bind native label for $H$
\State initialize compiler operand map from $layout[H]$
\State compile loop body with ordinary lazy operands
\ForAll{backedge stubs recorded during body emission}
  \State read sources from the stub's saved operand map
  \State emit parallel moves to $layout[H]$
    \Comment{One predecessor's layout is not another's}
  \State jump to $H$
\EndFor
\end{sudocode}

Use loop parameters $(a,b,k)$ in three slots. Enter with
$(2,5,2)$; each iteration swaps $a,b$ and decrements $k$,
returning $10a+b$ when $k=0$. The slots at successive headers
must be $(2,5,2),(5,2,1),(2,5,0)$ and the final result is 25.
If both values remain in their old slots until the edge, a
sequential copy for the first swap makes both 5, so the eventual
result is 55. Also test initial $k=0$ to exercise entry without
any backedge and $k=1$ to distinguish the swapped result 52.

The first backedge in \Cref{fig:visual-251} exchanges values, not slots: the next visit to the header reads the same three locations in the original order.

