The invariant at the native header is that the canonical slots
represent the bytecode parameters in order, whichever path arrived.
This layout trades extra edge moves for immediate code generation.
A later tier can keep selected parameters in registers once it has
a budget for analyzing all incoming states. Track the emitted moves
on each edge separately when comparing the two tiers.

\section{Tier transitions and shared machinery}

Izawa, Masuhara and Bolz-Tereick's \emph{Two-level Just-in-Time Compilation with One Interpreter and One Engine} presents adaptive RPython: threaded code generation is the first compilation tier, and ordinary tracing the optimizing tier. The former keeps calls to interpreter operations and connects control flow; the latter can optimize through the interpreter operations (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-253}). Xu and Kjolstad's \emph{Deegen: A JIT-Capable VM Generator for Dynamic Languages} makes a different trade: bytecode semantics written as C++ functions drive generation of an interpreter, a native baseline and inter-tier transitions. Titzer's Wasm case starts with static stack types; a dynamic-language generator additionally needs fast tagged cases, slow paths and runtime feedback. Sharing definitions reduces duplicated implementation, but agreement between two tiers generated from the same faulty definition is not an independent correctness test.

Dossier Chapters~02 and~08 suggest selecting among representations and compiling on demand. A concrete implementation of the blueprint's execution plane uses an atomic dispatch cell per function, plus distinct entries for existing activations (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-255}). Publish only a complete code-and-metadata bundle whose revision still matches. Keep the old bundle pinned while any active frame can use it. The queue in the example must coalesce duplicate requests and permit interpretation to continue if compilation has not started or was rejected. Choosing several representations is established practice; the dossier's more general policy for switching among them is a design to evaluate, not evidence that more tiers are better.

\section{Measuring the latency dividend}

As an illustrative synchronous case, if baseline preparation takes \(80\,\mu\mathrm{s}\) and saves \(0.4\,\mu\mathrm{s}\) per future call, it breaks even after 200 calls, not counting the 64 calls already interpreted. If preparation is asynchronous, count only calls after installation in that saving, and measure compiler CPU and queue contention separately. Run fresh-process workloads with many one-shot functions, a small set of frequently called functions, and a single long-running loop. For the last case, call-only counting is intentionally insufficient. Compare interpreter-only, eager baseline and demand baseline using time to first result, total elapsed time, and installed bytes including metadata. An engine that improves the median while delaying the slowest first requests has not necessarily met its latency contract.
